00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:28.980
Right, now for those Deadpool movie facts...

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:35.420
To produce Deadpool's scarred look, make-up
department head Bill Corso created thin, semi-transparent

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:39.510
silicone prosthetics that showed muscles,
blood and tissue under the skin.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:43.969
It took around four hours to get Ryan Reynolds
ready and he wore ten of those super-thin

00:00:43.969 --> 00:00:48.050
silicone prosthetics on his head and, for
one scene, even had them applied all over

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.129
his body.

00:00:49.129 --> 00:00:54.150
According to Reynolds, the prosthetics felt
like wearing a wet diaper on his face!

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:58.530
The character of Colossus, who's super-strong
and super-tall, is mostly computer-generated

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.739
with the aid of a motion capture performance.

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:05.810
And the fact his steel-like armoured form
is completely reflective was quite a challenge

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:07.020
for the filmmakers.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:11.180
To solve the problem, they used a 3-D camera
system to capture all the action that takes

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:12.479
place around Colossus.

00:01:12.479 --> 00:01:15.780
Then they placed that action onto Colossus's
body as a reflection.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.070
So, for example, when Deadpool runs around
Colossus, we can see the reflection of Deadpool

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.490
on his mutant ally's metal body.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:28.079
Speaking of Colossus, actor Daniel Cudmore
who played the character in X-Men 2, The Last

00:01:28.079 --> 00:01:31.750
Stand, and Days Of Future Past, was offered
the part for Deadpool, but turned it down

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:36.140
as not only is the character computer-generated
but also the filmmakers wanted to use another

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:37.590
actor's voice for the part.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.950
In the end, Colossus is played by three actors
in Deadpool.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:45.080
Andre Tricoteux did the motion capture performance,
Greg LaSalle did the facial motion capture,

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:49.189
and Stefan Kapicic provided the voice of Colossus.

00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:53.369
When it came to Deadpool's fight scenes, the
filmmakers wanted to combine bad-ass stunts

00:01:53.369 --> 00:01:54.640
with playful wise-cracking.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:59.790
So, Ryan Reynolds took cues from none other
than heavyweight boxing great Muhammad Ali,

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:04.220
not only in terms of Ali's moves in the ring
but also with regards to Ali's technique of

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:07.720
trash-talking his opponents both before and
during a match.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:12.360
In fact, actor Ed Skrein has said that the
clashes between his character Ajax and Deadpool

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:17.470
in the movie are reminiscent of Muhammad Ali
versus Joe Frazier.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.940
Stunt coordinator Philip Silvera and his team
have created a hybrid style for scenes where

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.250
Deadpool fights with his signature katanas.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:28.290
The style mixes 'tactical thinking, Japanese
and Chinese sword work', and the kind of strikes

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.550
used in Filipino martial arts.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:34.590
And speaking of Deadpool's katanas, Ryan Reynolds
has said on Twitter that he's named them Bea

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:38.641
and Arthur, presumably because in the comic
books the Merc with a Mouth just happens to

00:02:38.641 --> 00:02:44.660
be a huge fan of actress Bea Arthur who starred
in TV comedy The Golden Girls.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:48.920
Ryan Reynolds spent six months in the gym
getting beefed up to play Deadpool, and actually

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.410
threw his back out on the first day of training!

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:56.340
But by the time he came to wear his costume,
his muscles were so big that they had to remove

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:57.960
the muscle layer in the suit.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.440
Many superhero suits have a polyurethane-type
muscle suit underneath to add definition on

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:07.170
camera, but according to director Tim Miller
and Ryan Reynolds when Reynolds put on the

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:12.320
suit with the muscle layer it looked over
the top, so they removed it to give Reynolds

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.660
more of the classic Deadpool look.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:19.590
When Reynolds finished filming Deadpool, memories
of the shoot weren't the only thing he took

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:20.590
with him.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:25.090
In fact, the actor swiped one of his Deadpool
costumes, which he reckons he more than earned

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:29.620
after waiting 10 years to finally make the
movie.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:33.450
Deadpool may be R-rated, but apparently there
was still some stuff that was deemed just

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:36.290
too offensive to make it into the final cut.

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:41.380
One particular bar scene featuring Ryan Reynolds
and TJ Miller as Deadpool and his pal Weasel

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:46.330
was cut down because, according to director
Tim Miller, it went 'too far' and was particularly

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:48.650
mean and offensive to so many people.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:52.730
But even the final cut of the movie proved
too much for China, where the movie's been

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:58.350
refused permission to screen in cinemas due
to its violence, nudity and graphic language.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:03.050
The character of Negasonic Teenage Warhead,
who's played by Brianna Hildebrand, was created

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:07.920
by comic book writer Grant Morrison and named
after the song Negasonic Teenage Warhead by

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.570
the band Monster Magnet.

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:14.350
Warhead first appeared in 2001 in the New
X-Men comic books.

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:17.840
Deadpool screenwriters Rhett Reese and Paul
Wernick said they were given a pre-approved

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:21.630
list of characters they could use for the
movie and as soon as they saw the name Negasonic

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:25.400
Teenage Warhead they just had to use it!

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:30.030
Former mixed martial artist Gina Carano certainly
isn't afraid of doing many of her own stunts

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:35.870
in movies like Haywire and Deadpool, but there's
one thing she does absolutely detest and that's

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:36.870
spiders.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:42.230
While filming a fight scene between Colossus
and Carano's character Angel Dust, the MMA-star-turned-actress

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:46.310
spent a considerable amount of time getting
thrown around in the dirt at a junkyard, getting

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:48.300
the 'messiest' she's ever been in a brawl.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:53.180
But the main thing on her mind throughout
was whether the crew had done a spider check

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:54.230
before filming!

